course: dictionary
lesson: 2228
title: Covering: one word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Covering is a word with more than one meaning, so the context tells you which one you need. A covering is a layer that lies on or over something, like «a light covering of snow» or «a covering of fur». Covering can also be a verb meaning putting something over, protecting it, or hiding it.
explain: «The plastic covering on a cable keeps the live wires from touching you.» Here covering means a protective layer. Ask yourself what it covers and why. The clue is «keeps the live wires from touching you», so the covering protects you.
explain: At school, «covering a topic» means teaching it, and a cover teacher is covering for someone who is away. «Covering for someone» can also mean hiding their mistake, which is different and not always right. If someone has been hurt, covering it up is never right, because the hurt person needs grown-ups to know, so always tell a grown-up you trust.
fact: A covering is a layer that lies on or over something else.
fact: «A light covering of snow» means a thin layer of snow on the ground.
fact: «Covering a topic» means teaching it, and a cover teacher stands in for a teacher who is away.
fact: «Covering for someone» can mean hiding someone's mistake, and hiding an injury is never right.
fact: Never run cables under rugs, and never use frayed cables or broken plugs, because the plastic covering is what keeps live wires from touching you.
exercise: «The ground was hidden under a light covering of snow.» What is the covering here? | A tall tree | A loud noise | A kind of sweet | * A thin layer lying on top
exercise: A cover teacher is covering for someone who is away. What is the cover teacher doing? | Painting the classroom walls | Lending out library books | * Standing in for someone who is away | Hiding under a desk
exercise: «The plastic covering on a cable keeps the live wires from touching you.» Why is the covering there? | To make the cable heavier | To hide the colour of the cable | * To protect you from the live wires | To make the cable longer
test: What does «covering a topic» mean at school? | Drawing a picture of it | Forgetting it | Hiding it under a rug | * Teaching it
test: Someone you know has been hurt and asks you to keep it quiet. What should you do? | * Tell a grown-up you trust straight away | Cover it up with a blanket and say nothing | Wait a week to see if it gets better | Keep the secret so nobody gets in trouble
exam: In «the plastic covering on a cable», what is the covering's job? | Making the cable look nice | Making the cable heavier | Holding the cable up | * Keeping live wires from touching you
